  "answer_core": "Slow the situation down and make safety the first priority without blowing up trust. Tell your co‑parent enough to coordinate calmly on a safety plan, not to assign blame. Before deciding whether to report, talk with a therapist, RAINN, or a lawyer who understands mandatory‑reporting rules so you know what your options are.\n\nKeep your daughter’s sense of safety and openness central, but set clear boundaries: no private or disappearing‑message apps, no contact with this older person while you sort things out, and you quietly monitor for any workarounds. A gentle pause on phone use is okay if you explain it as protection rather than punishment, but avoid a sudden hard shutdown unless a tripwire fires.\n\nEscalate immediately if any of these happen: the older person asks to meet; sends or requests sexual images; threatens her; she says she feels pressured or scared; you find the contact has shifted to hidden channels after boundaries were set; or you see signs the person is pursuing other minors. If any tripwire hits, stop direct contact, save evidence, and get professional guidance right away.\n\nMeanwhile, keep talking with your daughter in a steady, non‑interrogating way. Emphasize that she isn’t in trouble, that adults sometimes hide their intentions, and that you are working with her—not against her—to keep her safe.",
